WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.610
Hi, so my name is
Daniel Jackson.

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:06.120
I'm a Professor in EECS
and a researcher in CSAIL.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.460
And I'm going to talk about
how to design software.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.820
So here are the two questions
I'm going to start with.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.040
First of all, what does
innovation in software

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:14.640
look like?

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.910
And secondly, what makes a
software product successful?

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.540
So let's look at some
scenarios of success,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.510
and I use this word
scenarios advisedly.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.420
So let's start with an example
of a great success, Tim

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.100
Berners-Lee's World Wide Web.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.250
So what was novel about the web?

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.460
So let me tell you
what wasn't novel.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.770
First of all, the technology
certainly wasn't novel.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.850
If you look at the technology
that Tim developed,

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.530
his first prototype
of the web on,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.690
it was pretty advanced
stuff even for back then.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.730
He had almost a gigabyte
on his hard disk.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.590
He had this fancy
[INAUDIBLE] computer.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.440
You're blocking the screen.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.607
Oh, I'm blocking the screen.

00:00:51.607 --> 00:00:53.050
Thank you.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.100
Where shall I stand then?

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:54.850
How about over here?

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:55.375
Is that better?

00:00:55.375 --> 00:00:55.875
[INAUDIBLE]

00:00:55.875 --> 00:00:57.670
Great.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.910
So it had a built in
ethernet connectivity,

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.710
so all the crucial elements
of functionality were there.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:07.960
So technology was certainly
not an innovation.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.300
What about the
fundamental ideas?

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.430
Well, the idea of
hyperlinks, well, that

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.760
had been there in hypercard
in Bill Atkinson's product

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.080
in 1987.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.973
In fact, hypertext itself
had gone back many years

00:01:20.973 --> 00:01:22.390
before that was
invented, probably

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.280
in 1967 in an editing system
that Ted Nelson and Andries van

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.050
Dam built.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.720
Arguably, the whole notion
of a hyperlink collection

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.990
of documents goes back
to Vannevar Bush's paper

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:38.980
in The Atlantic in 1945 where
he describes this fantastic

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:43.630
machine called the Memex, which
was a kind of mass of knowledge

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.880
that you could
navigate by taking

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.070
an elaborate path through
this labyrinth, and so on.

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.200
So these ideas weren't new.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.870
The idea of markup wasn't new.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.750
HTML was a new markup
language, but it

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.520
was based on SGML,
which had already

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.070
existed for several years.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.362
And finally, the idea of moving
files across the network,

00:02:04.362 --> 00:02:05.570
well, that wasn't new either.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.330
The file transfer protocol
was very well established.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:10.400
And, at the time of
the World Wide Web,

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.610
people were already downloading
papers from each other's sites,

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:14.490
and so on.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.750
So what was new?

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.590
So I'm going to argue that
something incredibly simple

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:22.130
was new, and it was in
the scenario by which

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:23.900
this downloading happened.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.420
So when you used an
FTP server, here's

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.740
basically what you had to do.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.320
You had to identify
your server as.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.240
You had to open a
connection to it.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.080
You had to set a directory,
find the right directory

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.310
inside that server.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.190
And then you could request
the particular file,

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:41.600
any number of files you wanted.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.892
And then you could close your
connection to the server.

00:02:43.892 --> 00:02:45.770
And the way I'm
describing this scenario,

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.560
these red bits are all
the annoying bits, right,

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.630
because you just
wanted those files.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:53.148
So why did you have to do
all these preliminary things

00:02:53.148 --> 00:02:55.190
of going to the server
and opening the connection

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:57.060
and closing the
connection, and so on?

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.270
So Tim's solution
was, well, why not

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:01.460
simply have a single
request that says,

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.850
get file F1 at server S?

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.560
And of course, this
idea in retrospect,

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.770
like a lot of great ideas,
is completely obvious.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:12.875
And of course, that thing
there, that address,

00:03:12.875 --> 00:03:15.440
S/F1 is just a URL.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.030
And so what this means is that
the real innovation of the web

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.770
was, in fact, the URL.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:25.820
It was the idea of having
persistent names for resources

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:30.380
and being able to request the
resource with a particular name

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.670
in one step.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.970
OK, second scenario, the iPod.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:36.920
What was novel about the iPod?

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.560
Well, of course, it was
a super slick device.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.350
But its design was
not its novelty.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.210
I'm sure many of you
have seen this before.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.400
It's been pointed out
many times famously,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.010
that Jony Ive's beautiful
design of the iPod

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:53.110
was based on Dieter Rams' design
of a pocket transistor radio

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.900
many years before, which
looks almost identical.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.020
It wasn't the technology either.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:03.410
Steve Jobs talked about
riding the wave of technology,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:08.300
and he was certainly absolutely
astute to technology advances.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.517
And in fact, there
was this Toshiba drive

00:04:10.517 --> 00:04:12.350
that came out just
before the iPod came out,

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.030
and he wanted to use that drive.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.670
And he also wanted to have
a really fast connector

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:20.060
so that you could upload your
stuff to your iPod really fast.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.480
But in fact, these were
just incremental advances.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:26.270
And way before the
iPod, there was a device

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.850
built by Digital called
the Personal Jukebox, which

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:29.720
was not much bigger.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.580
And it had a five
gigabyte drive.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:34.550
And so technology was
not what was responsible.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:36.680
So what was responsible?

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:39.470
Again, a fundamental
change in the scenario.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:44.030
So the way that you used an
mp3 player prior to the iPod

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.880
was at first you'd have to
do these nasty red things.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.080
You had to download a pirated
track with Napster or whatever,

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.450
or you had to rip your
CD collection, which

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.310
was a very tedious business.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:57.330
Then you had to upload
it to your iPod.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.410
And then finally, you
could play the thing.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.040
And what Steve Jobs
realized was that there

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.610
needed to be a simple,
streamlined scenario

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.090
in which you could buy a
song and play the song.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.800
You could play it
locally, and then you

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.750
could synchronize the
device on which you

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.870
bought the song with your iPod
and then play it on the iPod.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:18.150
And that means, of
course, that iTunes

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.340
was an important member
of the supporting

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.020
cast in this success.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.620
And iTunes came out shortly
after the iPod itself.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.520
And in fact, if you look at
the sales of the iPod, what

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.830
you really see is that
the sales of the iPod

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.980
really take off big
time after iTunes

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.630
has sold 100 million songs.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.637
So iTunes becomes
really well established,

00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:40.470
and it's really at that
point that everybody

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.500
decides that they need an iPod.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.600
Of course, the iPod disappears
into the shrouds of the past

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.630
because we have
iPhones now, which

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.850
are much more capable and
powerful and capacious devices,

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:55.350
and so on.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:58.080
I happen to think
that it's sad that we

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.010
don't have this lovely
little device anymore.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.430
I thought the shuffle
was super cute.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.460
And no one, as far as I know,
is making a phone this size.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:07.320
Phones just seem to
be getting bigger.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.420
Story number three, WhatsApp.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.370
So what was novel
about WhatsApp?

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.418
You can get the
pattern here, right?

00:06:13.418 --> 00:06:15.210
Well, it wasn't what
a lot of people think.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.850
It wasn't free texting, right,
because there were companies

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.580
that have produced free
texting and free phone

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.060
calls before that.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:25.290
And if you look at the
growth of WhatsApp,

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.110
you see that there was
a single big moment.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.200
And that big moment
was about there in 2011

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.640
when things really took off.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.610
And what happened at that
point was that WhatsApp

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.620
introduced group chat.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.690
So what was so
significant about--

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.960
and if you look
at the derivative,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:47.400
you can see much more
clearly how the group chat

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:50.130
feature corresponded to that
crucial point of explosion

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:50.850
of WhatsApp.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:54.480
A lot of people, by the way,
who only knew WhatsApp later,

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.490
think end-to-end
encryption was a big deal

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.780
because that came to be known
as a key feature of WhatsApp.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.530
But, as you can see,
end-to-end encryption

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:04.020
comes in much later when
the growth of WhatsApp

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.820
has really stabilized.

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.910
And it turns out that groups
were a really hot thing

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.290
back in 2011.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.440
Here's an example of an article
from The New York Times saying,

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.670
everybody's doing groups.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:16.050
It's the coolest thing on Earth.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:17.820
So what was so
cool about groups?

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.680
It seems so obvious
and natural to us now.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:23.130
Well, what was so
cool was that, before,

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.950
the notion of
groups, if you wanted

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:28.380
to engage a number of
people in a conversation,

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.510
then on every single
message you had

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.340
to add the recipients, right?

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.640
And every time someone
new was added to the call,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.340
you had to remember to
include them as well.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:42.480
In the group scenario, instead,
all that happens is they--

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.330
and they're represented here,
the other people by the yellow

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:46.050
boxes--

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:49.890
they simply spontaneously
decide to join the conversation.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.810
And all subsequent
messages are sent to them.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.820
And so what you're seeing
again is a dramatic change

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.030
in usability by switching
from a scenario that

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:05.160
has inconveniences in it
to a much simpler scenario.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.850
And it's that scenario
that's responsible

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.700
for the great uptake
of the technology.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.190
Story number four, Zoom.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.540
So this was fun, actually.

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.140
So I looked online to
see if I could find stats

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.210
about the growth
of Skype and Zoom.

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:25.560
And I found this
picture of Skype usage

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.550
from a site called statistica.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:29.550
And I thought, wow, that
looks a little strange.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.870
And then I realized there are
asterisks from 2018 onwards.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:38.039
And it turns out that that was
the projected growth of Skype.

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:38.970
It's still online.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.039
You can still find this picture.

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:45.270
As we all know, in fact, this
was not Skype's happy story.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.160
That, in fact, what
happened instead

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:51.300
was this, that basically Skype
and everybody else shouldered

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:52.560
off and went nowhere.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.600
And Zoom completely exploded.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.510
That's Zoom in red.

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:59.330
And that happened of course
at the time of COVID.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:01.760
So what was so
remarkable about Zoom?

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:12.690
So here's, by the way, this was
one of the first famous Zoom

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:13.813
meetings.

00:09:13.813 --> 00:09:15.480
Boris Johnson the
British prime minister

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.790
was so proud that
he was using Zoom

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.460
that he screenshotted
his Zoom call

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:24.810
and tweeted it out there
with the private Zoom

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:26.713
ID of the call
that, at the time,

00:09:26.713 --> 00:09:28.380
was available for
everybody in the world

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.230
to join his cabinet meetings.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.280
So this was really cool.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:36.390
But what was actually
new about video calls?

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:43.110
Well, just as David was saying,
that they had video calls back

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:46.230
on the internet 30 years
before Zoom or whatever,

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.570
actually turns out that
even prior to the internet

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:52.080
there was something called
the Picture Phone in 1964.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.470
And of course, there
was Skype in 2009.

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:59.340
So video calls were
not a novelty of Zoom.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:00.840
There were also webcams.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:03.540
You might think, well, how
could this actually work

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.680
if it wasn't
convenient to actually

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.150
have a webcam on your laptop?

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:11.230
But webcams have been
around since 1994,

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:13.630
and they were already
cheap devices.

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:15.560
Furthermore, you
might say, well,

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.280
maybe it wasn't so convenient
to ship video over the internet.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.238
Maybe there were a lot
of performance problems.

00:10:20.238 --> 00:10:26.560
Well, the H264 video codec was
invented or finalized in 2003.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:29.890
So again, the story was
that these things just

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:33.550
weren't convenient to use, that
if you wanted to use Skype,

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.230
this was typically
what you had to do.

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:38.200
First of all, you as the user
who wanted to talk to someone

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:39.550
had to sign up for the app.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:40.810
OK, not unreasonable.

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:43.120
But then the person
you were talking to--

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.550
and they're the red box-- they
had to sign up for the app

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:46.360
as well.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:48.550
You then place the
call, and then you

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:50.230
had to add them to the call.

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:53.080
And the problem there is that
if you wanted to add just one

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.690
person and they were
already using Skype

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:58.490
and you were talking to them
a lot, that was just fine.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:00.742
But if you wanted
to add 100 people,

00:11:00.742 --> 00:11:02.950
because you were running
some kind of Zoom conference

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:05.770
or whatever, this was
completely infeasible.

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:07.770
Then you could talk, and
then you could hang up.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:08.940
That part was fine.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:15.720
Now, to be fair, Skype did
have a group mechanism that

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.010
allowed you to define a group.

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.780
But even for the
group, you still

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.900
had to add everybody one
at a time to the group

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:24.510
before you could start the call.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:26.670
And of course, we
know what Zoom did.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:30.510
Zoom did something in a way more
complicated but that turns out

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.040
to be much simpler,
right, which is

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:36.180
that asynchronously, when you
create the meeting, what you do

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.520
is you get a link from
creating the meeting.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.910
And you can then send that link
out of band by email or text

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.470
or on the phone or
whatever you want

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.000
to do to all the participants.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.660
And then they can join
in yellow asynchronously.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:51.660
So this idea I
believe was really

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.850
crucial to the success
of Zoom, and this

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:57.330
is why Zoom pushed
Skype out of the way.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:00.780
And if you look at the entire
history of video conferencing,

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:03.480
and there have been so many
different applications,

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.490
one of the interesting things is
that a company is not typically

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.050
mentioned in this history.

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:11.140
And that's the
company LogMeIn, which

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:13.150
had a product called join.me.

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:15.340
And they had in their
product, because they

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.740
were trying to do an
innovator's dilemma,

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:19.810
sort of compete with
the big players.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:22.840
They wanted to produce really
lightweight video conferencing.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:25.060
And they had this idea
of a meeting link.

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:28.210
And it's that idea of the
meeting link and the scenario

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.990
that I showed you that
gets adopted by Zoom.

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:36.400
In fact, Skype, realizing
how badly they needed it,

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.890
when they saw Zoom
using this concept,

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:40.270
they did it themselves.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:43.180
But they did it in April
2020 when they'd already

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:45.750
lost momentum to Zoom.

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:48.250
And because it wasn't the
central way of actually making

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:49.990
a Skype call, I
think it was called

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:53.590
Meet Now or something,
people barely even noticed

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:55.973
that Skype had added
this crucial feature.

00:12:55.973 --> 00:12:58.390
And then, by the way, this
goes to all the other products.

00:12:58.390 --> 00:13:01.390
And Microsoft Teams, a
couple of years later,

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:04.330
realizes that it needs
this scenario too.

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:07.810
OK, as if I haven't driven
the point home enough,

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:10.780
one more example, Calendly.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:14.560
So Calendly is being
called the four billion

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:17.350
DocuSign of scheduling,
a phenomenally successful

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:18.140
company.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:19.720
So how did it do it?

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:21.970
Well, the critical
idea of Calendly

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:24.070
was that you could
schedule yourself

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:25.780
for a meeting with
somebody else.

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:29.320
So what that means is that,
rather than the normal kind

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.900
of elaborate and
painful scenario

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:36.340
in which basically the person
you're trying to schedule with

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:37.625
records their availability.

00:13:37.625 --> 00:13:40.000
And you go to them, and you
request some slots, you know,

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:41.708
what you used to have
to do if you wanted

00:13:41.708 --> 00:13:43.150
to go and get a haircut, right?

00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:45.882
And then they say, well, I
have 5 o'clock on Wednesday.

00:13:45.882 --> 00:13:46.840
Will that work for you?

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:47.680
No, that won't work.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.360
Well, how about 10
o'clock on Thursday.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:50.470
Eventually, you find a slot.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:51.250
You select the slot.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:51.880
You confirm it.

00:13:51.880 --> 00:13:53.200
All this painful stuff.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:57.730
Instead, what Calendly realized
was that you could simply

00:13:57.730 --> 00:14:00.340
have the person providing
the slots record

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:01.660
their availability.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:04.000
And then the users,
the people who

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.220
are going to actually use those
slots, would just select a slot

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:10.620
and would automatically
get a confirmation for it.

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:13.280
So this idea of
self-scheduling, like a lot

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:14.960
of great ideas, and
just the same way

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:17.450
that Zoom's key idea was
not invented by Zoom,

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:20.570
this key idea was not
invented by Calendly.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:22.070
I'm not sure who
it was invented by.

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:26.120
But I think it was invented
by a company called Acuity.

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.870
And Acuity had actually built
a self-scheduling system

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:32.060
that, unlike
Calendly, was focused

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:34.250
on small businesses,
which is perhaps why they

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:36.560
didn't grow in the same way.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:39.800
And they came up with this
self-scheduling scenario.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:42.770
And Calendly then
basically simplifies it

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:46.670
and grows it to the
single proprietor market.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:50.150
And of course, that idea
then becomes universal.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.670
Google Calendar had
something called slots back

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:56.030
in 2011, which was a much
more cumbersome mechanism,

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.430
a bit more like
Skype with respect

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:01.820
to Zoom, where you could
share calendars with people.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:03.280
And you could enter--

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:05.040
you could coordinate meetings.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:06.930
But it didn't have
this nice feature

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:10.530
that a nonuser of the
calendar could sign up

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:11.700
on someone else's calendar.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.825
And they added that with
something called schedules

00:15:13.825 --> 00:15:15.690
in 2022, I believe.

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:20.640
So the crucial idea here is
that successful software design

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:26.430
often involves identifying a key
scenario that changes the way

00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:29.550
people use the system,
that eliminates pain points

00:15:29.550 --> 00:15:33.090
and constraints, and makes
the innovation just completely

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:33.990
irresistible.

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:36.030
And once you have
this new scenario,

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:37.860
you can't live without it.

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:42.000
To use a term that Merrick first
invented, it becomes a not not.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:45.810
It becomes not acceptable
to not have it, right?

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.510
Once you have group
chat, you just

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:50.340
will not want to send
messages in which you

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:52.200
have to list every
single recipient

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:53.700
and keep track of
all the recipients

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:55.620
on every single message.

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:57.990
But scenarios aren't
the whole story.

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:00.630
So if you think about Zoom,
for example, it's not just

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.580
the meeting protocol, right?

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.770
It's also, for example, that
you can have text chat on Zoom,

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:06.270
right?

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.520
So there are actually
multiple scenarios

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:09.870
going on at the same time.

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:13.260
And each of these scenarios
is its own kind of innovation,

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.130
and I call these concepts.

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:17.340
So the way I think
of a software product

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:21.600
is basically a composition
of multiple concepts each

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.970
characterized by
its key scenario.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:26.700
So Zoom, for example, has, of
course, the crucial meeting

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.040
concept, the one I described.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.060
Of course, it has
video channels as well,

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.210
which is so obvious I
didn't describe them.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:36.060
But it has all these
many other scenarios too.

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:38.970
And to use Zoom you
have to understand

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:42.240
each of these concepts and
its essential scenarios.

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.250
Some of these are novel.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:47.010
At least prior to Zoom, people
hadn't done the meeting concept

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.240
with the idea of the link.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:49.870
Some of them are
familiar things.

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:51.840
So chat, for example,
the idea of a chat room

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.130
goes back decades.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:54.900
And some of them are
completely basic,

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:58.860
like the fact of having
an authenticated user.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:01.768
Concepts, by the way,
are not features, right?

00:17:01.768 --> 00:17:03.060
People often ask this question.

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:04.977
Well, isn't this just
the notion of a feature?

00:17:04.977 --> 00:17:06.960
They're not features
because, first of all,

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.170
these concepts represent
complete coherent functions,

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:11.640
not pieces of a function.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:13.470
They're not aspects
like the idea

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:15.990
of encryption, which can be
sort of layered and applied

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:16.710
to anything.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:19.020
They're not individual
actions, like the feature

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:21.390
of being able to reassign
the host of a call.

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:23.520
And they're not user
interface features,

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:26.910
like, for example, the
provision of shortcuts.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:29.040
The other thing
that's important is

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:32.610
that concepts are truly
composable services,

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:35.790
and they don't rely on
any base functionality.

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:37.427
When people talk
about features, they

00:17:37.427 --> 00:17:39.510
have in mind that there's
some base functionality,

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:41.250
and the features are
like little tweaks

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:43.200
that sit on top of the
base functionality.

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:45.150
But that doesn't need
to be any layering here.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:48.510
These concepts are
things that you interact

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:50.400
with on an equal basis.

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:52.230
And there are no
dependencies between them,

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.480
which means that they can
be built independently.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.460
So how are these put together?

00:17:56.460 --> 00:17:58.950
Well, in short, the way
they're put together

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:00.240
is by synchronization.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.830
So all we need to
say, for example,

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:08.160
is that in the chat concept,
you join a chat in Zoom

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:12.510
when you start the meeting,
if you're the person creating

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:13.170
the meeting.

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:15.253
And if you're the person
not creating the meeting,

00:18:15.253 --> 00:18:17.460
you join the chat when
you join the meeting.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:19.470
And so this idea
of synchronization

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:20.880
allows us to get
away from having

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:24.300
any kind of procedure
call notion of one concept

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:26.650
being superior to another.

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:30.400
Now, I don't have a lot of
time to talk about this,

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:32.410
so I'll just say that
when you actually

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:35.650
come to design a concept,
there's a lot more to say

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:37.630
beyond just this scenario.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:39.610
And, in particular,
you might want

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:42.400
to consider details, like well,
which messages can the user

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:43.900
actually read in a chat?

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:46.370
And if you want to do that,
you might need, for example,

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:50.140
to decide whether or not a
user can view messages that

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:53.950
have been previously
displayed prior to them

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:55.120
actually joining the chat.

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:56.830
And for that, we need
to design the state

00:18:56.830 --> 00:18:58.747
and we need to be explicit
about these things.

00:18:58.747 --> 00:19:01.732
This, by the, way I believe is
actually a serious design flaw

00:19:01.732 --> 00:19:03.190
because it's quite
an inconvenience

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:06.200
when you join a Zoom
chat that you can't see,

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.420
for example, any preliminary
notes that were sent out.

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:14.180
So how many of these fundamental
concepts are there in a product

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:15.930
that you need to
actually differentiate it

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:16.800
from other products?

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:19.530
Well, some products just
have one key concept,

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.140
and I'd argue that Zoom
and WhatsApp are like that.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:25.440
Some products have a whole
huge number of concepts.

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:28.890
You consider something like
Photoshop or QuarkXPress

00:19:28.890 --> 00:19:31.440
that are just piles
of complex ideas

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:32.670
that are developed over time.

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:34.980
And some actually have
no new concepts at all.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:38.130
They basically just
combine existing concepts

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:41.580
in often a quite creative way.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:44.772
And sometimes they do it even by
taking-- this is Hacker News--

00:19:44.772 --> 00:19:46.980
they do it by taking a bunch
of concepts you've seen,

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:49.440
posts and comments and
sessions and upvotes

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:51.130
and favorites and
comments and so on,

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:53.160
but putting them together
in a way that's tuned

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:56.080
to a particular kind of domain.

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:57.840
So I want to say a
little bit about how

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:01.140
you apply all this stuff.

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:05.040
First of all, you can use it
to diagnose usability problems.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.800
Here's one of my favorite ones.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:12.640
Poor old Melania Trump, on
seeing a rather rude tweet

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:16.600
about her in which she was
made fun of for brushing away

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:21.160
her husband's hand in a famous
video at an airfield somewhere,

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:25.690
she rather unfortunately
liked this tweet.

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.240
And people were a little puzzled
about why she'd done this.

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:31.330
But in fact, it was not her
fault. It was Twitter's fault.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:34.750
And the problem was that Twitter
had actually played around

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:37.330
with the whole notion of
likes and had explained

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:40.750
in a completely incoherent way
what these likes were about.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:43.647
We know that at times the
star could be confusing.

00:20:43.647 --> 00:20:45.730
You might like a lot of
things, but not everything

00:20:45.730 --> 00:20:46.605
can be your favorite.

00:20:46.605 --> 00:20:48.010
What does this mean?

00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:50.860
The problem was that there
were two fundamental concepts

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:53.110
with different
scenarios at hand here.

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:55.510
One was upvote,
basically saying I

00:20:55.510 --> 00:20:58.390
want that item to be ranked
more and everybody can see that.

00:20:58.390 --> 00:21:02.170
And the other was bookmark,
save this for me to visit later.

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:03.920
And of course,
Melania Trump thought

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:05.920
she was dealing with
bookmark when, in fact, she

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:08.020
was dealing with upvote.

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:11.290
And that was the
nature of the problem.

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:15.070
And Twitter, in fact,
fixed it later in addition

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.260
to the like, which
was really the upvote.

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:20.620
They eventually, albeit
buried rather deeply because--

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:23.350
this gets back to David's
point about power and money--

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:25.510
because it doesn't serve
their financial interests.

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:29.020
They eventually provided
a bookmarking feature

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:31.600
in a deep inner menu
that allowed you,

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:36.940
strangely under sharing, to
share a tweet to yourself,

00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:39.803
which essentially allowed
you to save it for later.

00:21:39.803 --> 00:21:41.470
A second thing you
can do with this idea

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:43.350
is you can catalog
your concepts.

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.850
And I don't have a lot of time
to tell you about this except

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:48.010
to say that at Palantir
they've done this

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:51.340
because they noticed a kind of
entropic growth of concepts.

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:54.640
And so what they did was
they built in their ontology

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:57.490
a large graph of about
300 concepts used

00:21:57.490 --> 00:21:59.350
across all their products.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:01.810
And then they used
these to basically think

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:04.540
about their products across,
not only different divisions

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:07.570
and different roles,
but they used it also

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:12.010
to think about the career
path of product managers.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:14.080
So basically, they
gave product managers

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:16.120
ownership over
individual concepts

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:18.970
that crossed across
application boundaries.

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:22.600
So what that meant was these
product managers could ensure

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:27.070
that a particular concept was
actually deployed consistently

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:29.020
in different products.

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:32.920
And the impacts they anticipate
from that are basically

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:36.370
getting more reuse,
getting more consistency,

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:38.680
and basically having
greater alignment

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:41.740
of understanding across
the whole company.

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:44.230
You can get better reuse.

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:47.170
This is an example
from PowerPoint.

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:48.760
So those of you
who use PowerPoint,

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:51.010
I don't honestly understand
why anyone uses PowerPoint

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:54.070
if they have a Mac because
it's, to be honest, so

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:55.450
inferior to Keynote.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:57.070
And this is just one example.

00:22:57.070 --> 00:22:59.710
It has this completely
baroque and complicated notion

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:01.930
of sections, which
they invented,

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.840
which is a complicated
way of dividing your slide

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:08.230
deck into pieces, which has a
lot of complicated commands.

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:10.030
It's very hard to understand.

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:12.370
If you look at
Keynote, at all they

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:15.850
have instead is this nudge
to the right or the left.

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:17.410
And why is that so easy to use?

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:19.060
Because it's a tree outline.

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:21.670
And we're completely
familiar with that concept.

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:26.290
It's completely standard, and
Apple simply chose to reuse it.

00:23:26.290 --> 00:23:27.970
More modular implementation.

00:23:27.970 --> 00:23:30.160
If you break things
down into concepts,

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:33.730
then you can actually build the
concepts as separate modules.

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:36.490
And that's exactly what we teach
in our software development

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:38.140
class to our undergraduates.

00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:41.350
And my student Santiago
Perez de Rosso in his thesis

00:23:41.350 --> 00:23:45.890
a few years ago showed
that, if you do this,

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:49.090
you can actually prebuild
all the concepts in advance

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:52.030
and then build applications
by essentially just writing

00:23:52.030 --> 00:23:55.810
glue, HTML style glue
that basically glues

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:57.700
these concepts
together and produces

00:23:57.700 --> 00:24:00.970
the behavior of an
emerging application

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:04.090
from the composition
of familiar concepts.

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:07.030
And we're now doing
some new work on this.

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:09.040
I have a project with
Kevin Sullivan looking

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:11.110
weaving together
different concepts

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:15.340
and with an MENG and
UROPs of mine looking

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:18.250
at using LLMs, both
to generate concepts

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:20.740
and these synchronizations
between them.

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.980
This is just a screenshot
from Santiago's work

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:28.240
that shows across the top
a list of final projects

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:30.520
from our class and how
he was able basically

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:33.820
to build those projects from
a list of standard concepts

00:24:33.820 --> 00:24:36.470
going down the left-hand side.

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:39.560
And here I think is the final
thing I want to point out,

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:42.680
which is that, when you start
thinking about things in terms

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:46.010
of standard concepts,
something interesting happens,

00:24:46.010 --> 00:24:47.990
which is that you
start to look at things

00:24:47.990 --> 00:24:49.700
in a more general way.

00:24:49.700 --> 00:24:52.790
And that tends to make your
applications more flexible.

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.980
So the standard
concept design move

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:58.550
is that you start with a simple
use case, some requirement.

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:00.440
But you don't implement
that use case.

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:02.090
Instead, what you
do is you consider

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:03.930
generalizing it with a concept.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:07.610
So for example, you want to
break your slides into subsets.

00:25:07.610 --> 00:25:10.790
So you say, well, what
about tree outlines, right?

00:25:10.790 --> 00:25:13.670
Once you have tree outlines,
you have unbounded depth.

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:15.800
You have all kinds of
other features, right?

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:19.520
Another example, in
Hacker News, right, they

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:21.860
have a rule that you can't
downvote until you've

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:23.540
upvoted some number of times.

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:26.000
Well, you could just burn that
into the notion of upvoting.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.280
But much better you
could say, there's

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:31.040
a general concept
of karma here, which

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:33.860
is that you accrue points for
certain kinds of good behavior

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:35.660
that you can then spend, right?

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:39.030
And once you have that, you can
do much more flexible things.

00:25:39.030 --> 00:25:41.940
Suppose you want in your
app to send an email.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:43.770
You've got a question
and answer app,

00:25:43.770 --> 00:25:45.362
and you want to
email a questioner

00:25:45.362 --> 00:25:46.320
when there's an answer.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:49.500
Well, don't embed that in
the precise details, right?

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:52.470
Recognize that this is an
example of a notification.

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:54.932
And if you build a generic
notification concept,

00:25:54.932 --> 00:25:56.640
you can let the user
control what they're

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:59.640
going to be notified about.

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:02.370
How about in
Mailchimp, for example.

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:04.980
There's all this baroque stuff
about setting the default

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:08.340
paragraph style because
they don't have a style

00:26:08.340 --> 00:26:11.460
concept that we're familiar with
from Microsoft Word and pages,

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:13.270
and so on.

00:26:13.270 --> 00:26:16.930
OK, I'm going to skip this
in the interest of time,

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:19.480
but the key point
is that you get

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:22.330
much more powerful and
flexible and modular

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:23.435
systems building this way.

00:26:23.435 --> 00:26:25.060
I want to close
actually by showing you

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:27.940
something which I think is
even cooler than that, which

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:30.130
is that sometimes when
you put concepts together

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:32.290
you get a curious
kind of synergy.

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:34.810
So here's my favorite
example, which

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:40.450
is the composition of the trash
concept and the folder concept.

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:42.130
Now, this might
seem bizarre to you

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:44.050
because you might think, how
could they ever be separate?

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:45.700
But in fact, in
early implementations

00:26:45.700 --> 00:26:47.320
of the trash in
Microsoft Windows,

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:49.000
the trash was not a folder.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.370
And when you moved a folder,
when you deleted a folder,

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:54.070
it disaggregated it
into individual files.

00:26:54.070 --> 00:26:57.640
But by treating the folder,
the trash rather, as a folder,

00:26:57.640 --> 00:26:59.380
you get all kinds of benefits.

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:02.440
But it turns out that's
actually really a subtle design.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:04.600
And one of the reasons
it's a subtle design

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:07.960
is because the trash has to hold
items from multiple volumes.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:11.200
So it turns out that the trash
is the only folder that's

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:14.360
sortable by volume, which
is a kind of a weird thing.

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:16.610
Furthermore, a really
cool thing they did,

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:19.040
notice that in the Mac
there's no date deleted.

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:21.440
You don't need date
deleted if you've

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:24.545
got a date added metadata
field in every folder.

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:29.760
Here's another really
cool synergy in MOIRA,

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:35.130
which is this very nice email
system developed at MIT.

00:27:35.130 --> 00:27:37.020
You can basically
create a mailing list

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:39.060
by listing a bunch
of people's names.

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:41.160
And the question
was, what if you

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:44.340
want the mailing list to be
owned by more than one person,

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:45.015
right?

00:27:45.015 --> 00:27:47.640
So for example, you've got like
a professor and the professor's

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:48.990
assistant, and you
want them both to be

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:50.220
able to change the list.

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:53.820
Well, you could complicate
the design by saying,

00:27:53.820 --> 00:27:55.710
let's allow multiple owners.

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.210
But they did a
much cooler thing,

00:27:57.210 --> 00:27:59.910
which was they said, well,
let's let a list be an owner.

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:00.990
And what kind of list?

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:03.330
Well, how about a
mailing list, right?

00:28:03.330 --> 00:28:07.110
So this is a kind of beautiful
sort of eating your own tail.

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.390
It's not a game
without complications

00:28:09.390 --> 00:28:12.690
because it turns out that
this means, for example, you

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:17.010
have to be able to say
that there are some mailing

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:18.618
lists that are
not mailing lists,

00:28:18.618 --> 00:28:20.910
right, because you don't want
every administrative list

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:22.410
to be used as a mailing list.

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:25.440
And it also turns out there's
a really weird thing which

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:27.820
is that MOIRA
mailing lists allow

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:31.750
you to have email addresses of
people who aren't MIT users.

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:34.840
And if you create an admin
list, all of whose members

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:37.498
are nonusers, it
will let you do it.

00:28:37.498 --> 00:28:39.040
But you're actually
now totally hosed

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:43.750
because now nobody can
administer that list.

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:46.690
My final example I
think labels in Gmail.

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:49.090
So here's again a beautiful
example of a synergy.

00:28:49.090 --> 00:28:51.160
We've got the notion
of a label, which

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:53.830
is a simple concept
for filtering things.

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:56.710
When you click on
that sent button

00:28:56.710 --> 00:28:59.410
to see the sent messages,
all you're seeing

00:28:59.410 --> 00:29:02.890
are the messages that have had
a sent label attached to them.

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:05.140
And the beauty of this
scheme is that now,

00:29:05.140 --> 00:29:08.200
for example, if I want to
search for all the items

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:10.930
that are labeled to do
and are in the trash,

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:13.720
I don't need special features
for saying what's in the trash.

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:16.060
I can just use this
trash label that

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:17.680
was automatically attached.

00:29:17.680 --> 00:29:22.330
And the mother of all
synergies is Photoshop.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:24.640
And I think I'm
running out of time,

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:29.050
so I'm going to leave
for offline discussion

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:31.390
to tell you about
all my enthusiasm

00:29:31.390 --> 00:29:35.230
for how amazing it is that
Photoshop managed essentially

00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:40.710
to make selections and masks
and channels all the same thing.

00:29:40.710 --> 00:29:42.500
So the takeaways from this.

00:29:42.500 --> 00:29:45.893
Mitch Kapoor said, "When
you go to design a house,

00:29:45.893 --> 00:29:47.060
you don't go to an engineer.

00:29:47.060 --> 00:29:48.060
You go to an architect."

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:50.840
And the reason for that is
that the criteria for what

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.030
makes a good building
fall outside the domain

00:29:53.030 --> 00:29:54.050
of engineering.

00:29:54.050 --> 00:29:56.180
And what we need
is people who can

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.240
think about the selection
of the various components

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:00.530
and, of course, their shaping.

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:02.580
And that's what
software designers do.

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:04.310
So what I've
suggested is a new way

00:30:04.310 --> 00:30:06.860
to think about software
in which scenarios

00:30:06.860 --> 00:30:09.260
point to the authentic
demand that you need.

00:30:09.260 --> 00:30:12.350
And, by identifying
constraints and pain points,

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:14.060
you can come up
with new scenarios

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:16.310
in which these
scenarios sometimes

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:17.630
characterize applications.

00:30:17.630 --> 00:30:20.840
But more often, they
characterize the concepts

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:24.440
that are the central
components of the applications.

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:27.770
Applications are then
compositions of these concepts.

00:30:27.770 --> 00:30:29.570
And, by thinking
in those terms, you

00:30:29.570 --> 00:30:32.360
get greater flexibility
and reuse and modularity.

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:35.840
And that's what my book
is about, shameless plug.

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:38.150
And on my website,
you'll find lots

00:30:38.150 --> 00:30:42.120
of blog posts and tutorials
and rants and why I think

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:43.680
actually Zoom is,
in some respects,

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:45.420
a little broken after all--

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.610
the notion of dark concepts.

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:50.430
And people often ask
about conceptual models,

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:52.590
and I talk a little
bit about why

00:30:52.590 --> 00:30:56.030
I think conceptual models are
not the same thing as concepts.

